# Convai documentation authoring

This repository is Convai's public GitBook documentation. When creating or editing any
Markdown page here, follow `.convai-docs/rules/`. Those files are the same rules the Convai
documentation plugin applies in Claude Code.

Read `.convai-docs/rules/SKILL.md` first — it routes you to everything else. For any page,
always read `references/writing-standards.md`, `references/structure.md`,
`references/gitbook-blocks.md`, and `references/diataxis.md`, then the pack in
`.convai-docs/rules/packs/` for whatever the page is about. Start from the matching
skeleton in `.convai-docs/rules/templates/`.

The rules that break pages most often:

- Never invent an API name, field, default, limit, behavior, console message, or UI label.
  If a fact is missing, stop and ask. This one is absolute.
- No body `#` heading. Start with a headingless lead paragraph; sections start at `##`.
- No `## Overview` or `## Introduction`. Sentence case headings. One Diataxis mode per page.
- `description` frontmatter: one plain-English outcome sentence, 120-160 characters, no
  identifiers or backticks.
- Every code block declares a language. Never screenshot code, logs, or configuration.
- Use `<code class="expression">space.vars.name</code>` for synchronized values. Never
  `{{ ... }}` — GitBook renders that as literal text.
- Keep `SUMMARY.md` sidebar labels identical to each page's `title`.
- Work on `staging`. Prepare changes and stop; a human commits, pushes, and merges.

Before handing off, run:

```bash
python .convai-docs/rules/tools/check_page.py "<page.md>" --summary SUMMARY.md
```

Fix every ERROR, then walk `.convai-docs/rules/references/quality-checklist.md` item by item.
